                "2": {
                    "name": "text_block",
                    "id": 311,
                    "position_x": 902,
                    "position_y": 45,
                    "width": 768,
                    "height": 1666,
                    "visibility": "show_all",
                    "draggable": true,
                    "presentation_state": "show_all",
                    "properties": {
                        "0": {
                            "name": "block",
                            "value": "The short-time Fourier transform: cuts a signal into short overlapping windows and finds the frequencies in each. The result is a picture with frequency one way and time the other, bright where there is energy - a spectrogram - showing how a sound or a movement changes: a pitch rising, a rhythm speeding up, a tremor that comes and goes.\n\nIt runs on the GPU when the signal is there, so long audio buffers are cheap.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.stft <window> <hop>\nDefaults: 256 samples, hop 0 (a quarter of the window).\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nsignal:\nA line of samples, or several (channels x samples), each analysed separately. Every input sends.\n\nsample rate (float box, also an inlet):\nSamples per second: only used to label the frequencies output.\n\nwindow (int box, also an inlet):\nSamples per window. Longer windows tell frequencies apart more finely but blur when things happened; the result has window / 2 + 1 frequency bins.\n\nhop (int box, also an inlet):\nSamples from the start of one window to the next; 0 means a quarter of the window.\n\nwindow shape:\nAn option, under the node: hann (the default), hamming, blackman, or rectangular (no shaping, which smears frequencies).\n\noutput:\nAn option, under the node: magnitude (the default), power (magnitude squared), decibels (10 log10 of the power), or complex - the full result, which t.istft can turn back into a signal.\n\ncenter:\nAn option, under the node. On (the default), each window is centred on its moment, with the signal mirrored at the ends.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nspectrogram:\nFrequency bins x windows (channels x bins x windows for several channels): low frequencies in the first row, so a heat_map shows them at the top.\n\nfrequencies:\nThe frequency of each bin, in cycles per second at the sample rate. Sent first.\n\nThe answer stays on the input's device: a tensor on the GPU is worked on there and comes out there.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nA chirp - a wave whose pitch rises steadily (made in the make_chirp_4096 subpatcher;\ndouble-click it to look inside) - as decibels. Time runs left to right and frequency top to bottom: a bright line running down from the top left as the pitch rises.\n\nRELATED:\nt.istft turns a complex spectrogram back into a signal. scipy.spectrogram is the scipy version. t.rfft gives the spectrum of the whole signal at once. t.unfold cuts the windows without transforming them.",
                            "value_type": "str"
                        },
                        "1": {
                            "name": "lock",
                            "value": true,
                            "value_type": "bool"
                        },
                        "2": {
                            "name": "width",
                            "value": 760,
                            "value_type": "int"
                        },
                        "3": {
                            "name": "height",
                            "value": 1650,
                            "value_type": "int"
                        },
                        "4": {
                            "name": "text_size",
                            "value": "30",
                            "value_type": "str"
                        }
                    },
                    "text": "The short-time Fourier transform: cuts a signal into short overlapping windows and finds the frequencies in each. The result is a picture with frequency one way and time the other, bright where there is energy - a spectrogram - showing how a sound or a movement changes: a pitch rising, a rhythm speeding up, a tremor that comes and goes.\n\nIt runs on the GPU when the signal is there, so long audio buffers are cheap.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.stft <window> <hop>\nDefaults: 256 samples, hop 0 (a quarter of the window).\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nsignal:\nA line of samples, or several (channels x samples), each analysed separately. Every input sends.\n\nsample rate (float box, also an inlet):\nSamples per second: only used to label the frequencies output.\n\nwindow (int box, also an inlet):\nSamples per window. Longer windows tell frequencies apart more finely but blur when things happened; the result has window / 2 + 1 frequency bins.\n\nhop (int box, also an inlet):\nSamples from the start of one window to the next; 0 means a quarter of the window.\n\nwindow shape:\nAn option, under the node: hann (the default), hamming, blackman, or rectangular (no shaping, which smears frequencies).\n\noutput:\nAn option, under the node: magnitude (the default), power (magnitude squared), decibels (10 log10 of the power), or complex - the full result, which t.istft can turn back into a signal.\n\ncenter:\nAn option, under the node. On (the default), each window is centred on its moment, with the signal mirrored at the ends.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nspectrogram:\nFrequency bins x windows (channels x bins x windows for several channels): low frequencies in the first row, so a heat_map shows them at the top.\n\nfrequencies:\nThe frequency of each bin, in cycles per second at the sample rate. Sent first.\n\nThe answer stays on the input's device: a tensor on the GPU is worked on there and comes out there.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nA chirp - a wave whose pitch rises steadily (made in the make_chirp_4096 subpatcher;\ndouble-click it to look inside) - as decibels. Time runs left to right and frequency top to bottom: a bright line running down from the top left as the pitch rises.\n\nRELATED:\nt.istft turns a complex spectrogram back into a signal. scipy.spectrogram is the scipy version. t.rfft gives the spectrum of the whole signal at once. t.unfold cuts the windows without transforming them."
                },
